1
00:00:04,032 --> 00:00:08,665
THE Bennets were engaged to dine with the Lucases; and again, during the chief of the day, was Miss

2
00:00:08,665 --> 00:00:13,566
Lucas so kind as to listen to Mr. Collins. Elizabeth took an opportunity of thanking her.

3
00:00:13,566 --> 00:00:18,899
“It keeps him in good humour,” said she, “and I am more obliged to you than I can express.”

4
00:00:19,865 --> 00:00:24,600
Charlotte assured her friend of her satisfaction in being useful, and that it amply repaid her

5
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:29,600
for the little sacrifice of her time. This was very amiable; but Charlotte’s kindness

6
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:35,066
extended farther than Elizabeth had any conception of:—its object was nothing less than to secure her

7
00:00:35,066 --> 00:00:40,333
from any return of Mr. Collins’s addresses, by engaging them towards herself. Such was

8
00:00:40,333 --> 00:00:44,966
Miss Lucas’s scheme; and appearances were so favourable, that when they parted at night,

9
00:00:44,966 --> 00:00:49,466
she would have felt almost sure of success if he had not been to leave Hertfordshire so

10
00:00:49,466 --> 00:00:55,100
very soon. But here she did injustice to the fire and independence of his character; for

11
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:59,966
it led him to escape out of Longbourn House the next morning with admirable slyness, and hasten

12
00:00:59,966 --> 00:01:04,865
to Lucas Lodge to throw himself at her feet. He was anxious to avoid the notice of his cousins,

13
00:01:04,865 --> 00:01:09,299
from a conviction that, if they saw him depart, they could not fail to conjecture his design,

14
00:01:09,299 --> 00:01:14,600
and he was not willing to have the attempt known till its success could be known likewise; for,

15
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:19,700
though feeling almost secure, and with reason, for Charlotte had been tolerably encouraging, he

16
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:24,966
was comparatively diffident since the adventure of Wednesday. His reception, however, was of the most

17
00:01:24,966 --> 00:01:29,765
flattering kind. Miss Lucas perceived him from an upper window as he walked towards the house,

18
00:01:29,765 --> 00:01:35,233
and instantly set out to meet him accidentally in the lane. But little had she dared to hope

19
00:01:35,233 --> 00:01:40,765
that so much love and eloquence awaited her there. In as short a time as Mr. Collins’s long speeches

20
00:01:40,765 --> 00:01:45,299
would allow, everything was settled between them to the satisfaction of both; and as they entered

21
00:01:45,299 --> 00:01:49,500
the house, he earnestly entreated her to name the day that was to make him the happiest of men;

22
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:54,365
and though such a solicitation must be waived for the present, the lady felt no inclination

23
00:01:54,365 --> 00:01:59,365
to trifle with his happiness. The stupidity with which he was favoured by nature must guard

24
00:01:59,365 --> 00:02:04,165
his courtship from any charm that could make a woman wish for its continuance; and Miss Lucas,

25
00:02:04,165 --> 00:02:07,799
who accepted him solely from the pure and disinterested desire of an establishment,

26
00:02:07,799 --> 00:02:14,000
cared not how soon that establishment were gained. Sir William and Lady Lucas were speedily applied

27
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:18,966
to for their consent; and it was bestowed with a most joyful alacrity. Mr. Collins’s

28
00:02:18,966 --> 00:02:22,399
present circumstances made it a most eligible match for their daughter,

29
00:02:22,399 --> 00:02:27,132
to whom they could give little fortune; and his prospects of future wealth were exceedingly

30
00:02:27,132 --> 00:02:32,265
fair. Lady Lucas began directly to calculate, with more interest than the matter had ever

31
00:02:34,799 --> 00:02:39,966
Bennet was likely to live; and Sir William gave it as his decided opinion, that whenever Mr. Collins

32
00:02:39,966 --> 00:02:44,899
should be in possession of the Longbourn estate, it would be highly expedient that both he and his

33
00:02:44,899 --> 00:02:50,399
wife should make their appearance at St. James’s. The whole family in short were properly overjoyed

34
00:02:50,399 --> 00:02:55,032
on the occasion. The younger girls formed hopes of coming out a year or two sooner than they

35
00:02:55,032 --> 00:02:59,000
might otherwise have done; and the boys were relieved from their apprehension of Charlotte’s

36
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:04,933
dying an old maid. Charlotte herself was tolerably composed. She had gained her point, and had time

37
00:03:04,933 --> 00:03:10,566
to consider of it. Her reflections were in general satisfactory. Mr. Collins, to be sure, was neither

38
00:03:10,566 --> 00:03:16,700
sensible nor agreeable: his society was irksome, and his attachment to her must be imaginary. But

39
00:03:16,700 --> 00:03:22,200
still he would be her husband. Without thinking highly either of men or of matrimony, marriage

40
00:03:22,200 --> 00:03:26,365
had always been her object: it was the only honourable provision for well-educated young women

41
00:03:26,365 --> 00:03:31,700
of small fortune, and, however uncertain of giving happiness, must be their pleasantest preservative

42
00:03:31,700 --> 00:03:37,632
from want. This preservative she had now obtained; and at the age of twenty-seven, without having

43
00:03:37,632 --> 00:03:42,865
ever been handsome, she felt all the good luck of it. The least agreeable circumstance in the

44
00:03:42,865 --> 00:03:47,233
business was the surprise it must occasion to Elizabeth Bennet, whose friendship she valued

45
00:03:47,233 --> 00:03:52,632
beyond that of any other person. Elizabeth would wonder, and probably would blame her; and though

46
00:03:52,632 --> 00:03:58,333
her resolution was not to be shaken, her feelings must be hurt by such a disapprobation. She

47
00:03:58,333 --> 00:04:03,365
resolved to give her the information herself; and therefore charged Mr. Collins, when he returned

48
00:04:03,365 --> 00:04:08,665
to Longbourn to dinner, to drop no hint of what had passed before any of the family. A promise

49
00:04:08,665 --> 00:04:14,000
of secrecy was of course very dutifully given, but it could not be kept without difficulty; for

50
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:19,200
the curiosity excited by his long absence burst forth in such very direct questions on his return,

51
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:25,165
as required some ingenuity to evade, and he was at the same time exercising great self-denial,

52
00:04:25,165 --> 00:04:31,032
for he was longing to publish his prosperous love. As he was to begin his journey too early on the

53
00:04:31,032 --> 00:04:35,700
morrow to see any of the family, the ceremony of leave-taking was performed when the ladies

54
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:39,799
moved for the night; and Mrs. Bennet, with great politeness and cordiality,

55
00:04:39,799 --> 00:04:44,100
said how happy they should be to see him at Longbourn again, whenever his other engagements

56
00:04:44,100 --> 00:04:48,600
might allow him to visit them. “My dear madam,” he replied,

57
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:54,066
“this invitation is particularly gratifying, because it is what I have been hoping to receive;

58
00:04:54,066 --> 00:04:58,399
and you may be very certain that I shall avail myself of it as soon as possible.”

59
00:04:59,299 --> 00:05:02,833
They were all astonished; and Mr. Bennet, who could by no means wish

60
00:05:02,833 --> 00:05:08,233
for so speedy a return, immediately said,— “But is there not danger of Lady Catherine’s

61
00:05:08,233 --> 00:05:12,365
disapprobation here, my good sir? You had better neglect your relations than run the

62
00:05:12,365 --> 00:05:17,399
risk of offending your patroness.” “My dear sir,” replied Mr. Collins,

63
00:05:17,399 --> 00:05:21,566
“I am particularly obliged to you for this friendly caution,

64
00:05:21,566 --> 00:05:27,132
and you may depend upon my not taking so material a step without her Ladyship’s concurrence.”

65
00:05:27,865 --> 00:05:32,365
“You cannot be too much on your guard. Risk anything rather than her displeasure;

66
00:05:32,365 --> 00:05:36,132
and if you find it likely to be raised by your coming to us again, which I should

67
00:05:36,132 --> 00:05:40,933
think exceedingly probable, stay quietly at home, and be satisfied that we shall take no offence.”

68
00:05:41,700 --> 00:05:48,433
“Believe me, my dear sir, my gratitude is warmly excited by such affectionate attention; and,

69
00:05:48,433 --> 00:05:51,600
depend upon it, you will speedily receive from me a letter of thanks

70
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:55,632
for this as well as for every other mark of your regard during my stay in Hertfordshire.

71
00:05:56,165 --> 00:06:01,933
As for my fair cousins, though my absence may not be long enough to render it necessary, I

72
00:06:01,933 --> 00:06:06,799
shall now take the liberty of wishing them health and happiness, not excepting my cousin Elizabeth.”

73
00:06:07,933 --> 00:06:12,733
With proper civilities, the ladies then withdrew; all of them equally surprised to find that he

74
00:06:12,733 --> 00:06:17,365
meditated a quick return. Mrs. Bennet wished to understand by it that he thought of paying his

75
00:06:17,365 --> 00:06:22,165
addresses to one of her younger girls, and Mary might have been prevailed on to accept him. She

76
00:06:22,165 --> 00:06:26,700
rated his abilities much higher than any of the others: there was a solidity in his reflections

77
00:06:26,700 --> 00:06:32,233
which often struck her; and though by no means so clever as herself, she thought that, if encouraged

78
00:06:32,233 --> 00:06:37,200
to read and improve himself by such an example as hers, he might become a very agreeable companion.

79
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:42,865
But on the following morning every hope of this kind was done away. Miss Lucas called soon after

80
00:06:42,865 --> 00:06:47,399
breakfast, and in a private conference with Elizabeth related the event of the day before.

81
00:06:47,399 --> 00:06:53,066
The possibility of Mr. Collins’s fancying himself in love with her friend had once occurred to

82
00:06:53,066 --> 00:06:57,899
Elizabeth within the last day or two: but that Charlotte could encourage him seemed almost as

83
00:06:57,899 --> 00:07:02,365
far from possibility as that she could encourage him herself; and her astonishment was consequently

84
00:07:02,365 --> 00:07:08,000
so great as to overcome at first the bounds of decorum, and she could not help crying out,—

85
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:12,799
“Engaged to Mr. Collins! my dear Charlotte, impossible!”

86
00:07:13,500 --> 00:07:17,466
The steady countenance which Miss Lucas had commanded in telling her story gave

87
00:07:17,466 --> 00:07:21,566
way to a momentary confusion here on receiving so direct a reproach; though,

88
00:07:21,566 --> 00:07:26,000
as it was no more than she expected, she soon regained her composure, and calmly replied,—

89
00:07:26,600 --> 00:07:31,466
“Why should you be surprised, my dear Eliza? Do you think it incredible that Mr. Collins should be

90
00:07:31,466 --> 00:07:35,399
able to procure any woman’s good opinion, because he was not so happy as to succeed with you?”

91
00:07:36,200 --> 00:07:41,066
But Elizabeth had now recollected herself; and, making a strong effort for it, was able to assure

92
00:07:41,066 --> 00:07:45,265
her, with tolerable firmness, that the prospect of their relationship was highly grateful to her,

93
00:07:45,265 --> 00:07:51,200
and that she wished her all imaginable happiness. “I see what you are feeling,” replied Charlotte;

94
00:07:51,200 --> 00:07:56,833
“you must be surprised, very much surprised, so lately as Mr. Collins was wishing to marry you.

95
00:07:57,365 --> 00:08:01,466
But when you have had time to think it all over, I hope you will be satisfied with what I have done.

96
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:07,566
I am not romantic, you know. I never was. I ask only a comfortable home; and,

97
00:08:07,566 --> 00:08:12,299
considering Mr. Collins’s character, connections, and situation in life, I am convinced that my

98
00:08:12,299 --> 00:08:16,500
chance of happiness with him is as fair as most people can boast on entering the marriage state.”

99
00:08:17,532 --> 00:08:23,966
Elizabeth quietly answered “undoubtedly;” and, after an awkward pause, they returned to the

100
00:08:23,966 --> 00:08:29,165
rest of the family. Charlotte did not stay much longer; and Elizabeth was then left to reflect on

101
00:08:29,165 --> 00:08:33,899
what she had heard. It was a long time before she became at all reconciled to the idea of

102
00:08:33,899 --> 00:08:38,899
so unsuitable a match. The strangeness of Mr. Collins’s making two offers of marriage within

103
00:08:38,899 --> 00:08:44,299
three days was nothing in comparison of his being now accepted. She had always felt that Charlotte’s

104
00:08:44,299 --> 00:08:49,032
opinion of matrimony was not exactly like her own; but she could not have supposed it possible that,

105
00:08:49,032 --> 00:08:53,466
when called into action, she would have sacrificed every better feeling to worldly

106
00:08:53,466 --> 00:08:59,700
advantage. Charlotte, the wife of Mr. Collins, was a most humiliating picture! And to the pang

107
00:08:59,700 --> 00:09:04,165
of a friend disgracing herself, and sunk in her esteem, was added the distressing conviction

108
00:09:04,165 --> 00:09:08,833
that it was impossible for that friend to be tolerably happy in the lot she had chosen.

109
00:09:11,365 --> 00:09:12,500
CHAPTER XXIII.

110
00:09:19,165 --> 00:09:23,000
ELIZABETH was sitting with her mother and sisters, reflecting on what she had heard,

111
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:28,165
and doubting whether she was authorized to mention it, when Sir William Lucas himself appeared,

112
00:09:28,165 --> 00:09:33,299
sent by his daughter to announce her engagement to the family. With many compliments to them,

113
00:09:33,299 --> 00:09:38,233
and much self-gratulation on the prospect of a connection between the houses, he unfolded the

114
00:09:38,233 --> 00:09:43,865
matter,—to an audience not merely wondering, but incredulous; for Mrs. Bennet, with more

115
00:09:43,865 --> 00:09:50,000
perseverance than politeness, protested he must be entirely mistaken; and Lydia, always unguarded

116
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:54,865
and often uncivil, boisterously exclaimed,— “Good Lord! Sir William, how can you tell

117
00:09:54,865 --> 00:09:57,966
such a story? Do not you know that Mr. Collins wants to marry Lizzy?”

118
00:09:58,500 --> 00:10:02,600
Nothing less than the complaisance of a courtier could have borne without anger such treatment:

119
00:10:02,600 --> 00:10:06,733
but Sir William’s good-breeding carried him through it all; and though he begged leave

120
00:10:06,733 --> 00:10:10,200
to be positive as to the truth of his information, he listened to all their

121
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:15,566
impertinence with the most forbearing courtesy. Elizabeth, feeling it incumbent on her to relieve

122
00:10:15,566 --> 00:10:19,566
him from so unpleasant a situation, now put herself forward to confirm his account,

123
00:10:19,566 --> 00:10:24,365
by mentioning her prior knowledge of it from Charlotte herself; and endeavoured to put a stop

124
00:10:24,365 --> 00:10:28,566
to the exclamations of her mother and sisters, by the earnestness of her congratulations to

125
00:10:28,566 --> 00:10:33,132
Sir William, in which she was readily joined by Jane, and by making a variety of remarks

126
00:10:33,132 --> 00:10:37,865
on the happiness that might be expected from the match, the excellent character of Mr. Collins, and

127
00:10:37,865 --> 00:10:43,100
the convenient distance of Hunsford from London. Mrs. Bennet was, in fact, too much overpowered to

128
00:10:43,100 --> 00:10:47,899
say a great deal while Sir William remained; but no sooner had he left them than her feelings found

129
00:10:47,899 --> 00:10:53,700
a rapid vent. In the first place, she persisted in disbelieving the whole of the matter; secondly,

130
00:10:53,700 --> 00:10:58,500
she was very sure that Mr. Collins had been taken in; thirdly, she trusted that they would never be

131
00:10:58,500 --> 00:11:03,665
happy together; and, fourthly, that the match might be broken off. Two inferences, however,

132
00:11:03,665 --> 00:11:08,466
were plainly deduced from the whole: one, that Elizabeth was the real cause of all the mischief;

133
00:11:08,466 --> 00:11:13,265
and the other, that she herself had been barbarously used by them all; and on these two

134
00:11:13,265 --> 00:11:18,600
points she principally dwelt during the rest of the day. Nothing could console and nothing appease

135
00:11:18,600 --> 00:11:23,765
her. Nor did that day wear out her resentment. A week elapsed before she could see Elizabeth

136
00:11:23,765 --> 00:11:28,632
without scolding her: a month passed away before she could speak to Sir William or Lady Lucas

137
00:11:28,632 --> 00:11:32,765
without being rude; and many months were gone before she could at all forgive their daughter.

138
00:11:33,365 --> 00:11:37,399
Mr. Bennet’s emotions were much more tranquil on the occasion, and such as

139
00:11:37,399 --> 00:11:42,066
he did experience he pronounced to be of a most agreeable sort; for it gratified him, he said,

140
00:11:42,066 --> 00:11:47,165
to discover that Charlotte Lucas, whom he had been used to think tolerably sensible, was as foolish

141
00:11:47,165 --> 00:11:52,765
as his wife, and more foolish than his daughter! Jane confessed herself a little surprised at the

142
00:11:52,765 --> 00:11:56,532
match: but she said less of her astonishment than of her earnest desire for their happiness;

143
00:11:56,532 --> 00:12:01,500
nor could Elizabeth persuade her to consider it as improbable. Kitty and Lydia were far

144
00:12:01,500 --> 00:12:06,566
from envying Miss Lucas, for Mr. Collins was only a clergyman; and it affected them in no other way

145
00:12:06,566 --> 00:12:12,066
than as a piece of news to spread at Meryton. Lady Lucas could not be insensible of triumph

146
00:12:12,066 --> 00:12:15,600
on being able to retort on Mrs. Bennet the comfort of having a daughter well married;

147
00:12:15,600 --> 00:12:20,600
and she called at Longbourn rather oftener than usual to say how happy she was, though

148
00:12:20,600 --> 00:12:25,333
Mrs. Bennet’s sour looks and ill-natured remarks might have been enough to drive happiness away.

149
00:12:26,066 --> 00:12:30,665
Between Elizabeth and Charlotte there was a restraint which kept them mutually silent on

150
00:12:30,665 --> 00:12:35,765
the subject; and Elizabeth felt persuaded that no real confidence could ever subsist between them

151
00:12:35,765 --> 00:12:41,000
again. Her disappointment in Charlotte made her turn with fonder regard to her sister, of whose

152
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:46,032
rectitude and delicacy she was sure her opinion could never be shaken, and for whose happiness she

153
00:12:46,032 --> 00:12:51,066
grew daily more anxious, as Bingley had now been gone a week, and nothing was heard of his return.

154
00:12:51,966 --> 00:12:56,966
Jane had sent Caroline an early answer to her letter, and was counting the days till she might

155
00:12:56,966 --> 00:13:01,933
reasonably hope to hear again. The promised letter of thanks from Mr. Collins arrived on Tuesday,

156
00:13:01,933 --> 00:13:06,665
addressed to their father, and written with all the solemnity of gratitude which a twelve-month’s

157
00:13:06,665 --> 00:13:11,066
abode in the family might have prompted. After discharging his conscience on that head,

158
00:13:11,066 --> 00:13:16,200
he proceeded to inform them, with many rapturous expressions, of his happiness in having obtained

159
00:13:16,200 --> 00:13:20,165
the affection of their amiable neighbour, Miss Lucas, and then explained that it was merely

160
00:13:20,165 --> 00:13:24,299
with the view of enjoying her society that he had been so ready to close with their kind wish

161
00:13:24,299 --> 00:13:28,333
of seeing him again at Longbourn, whither he hoped to be able to return on Monday fortnight;

162
00:13:28,333 --> 00:13:33,200
for Lady Catherine, he added, so heartily approved his marriage, that she wished it

163
00:13:33,200 --> 00:13:37,265
to take place as soon as possible, which he trusted would be an unanswerable argument with

164
00:13:37,265 --> 00:13:41,165
his amiable Charlotte to name an early day for making him the happiest of men.

165
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:47,399
Mr. Collins’s return into Hertfordshire was no longer a matter of pleasure to Mrs. Bennet. On

166
00:13:47,399 --> 00:13:52,265
the contrary, she was as much disposed to complain of it as her husband. It was very

167
00:13:52,265 --> 00:13:57,299
strange that he should come to Longbourn instead of to Lucas Lodge; it was also very inconvenient

168
00:13:57,299 --> 00:14:02,165
and exceedingly troublesome. She hated having visitors in the house while her health was so

169
00:14:02,165 --> 00:14:07,466
indifferent, and lovers were of all people the most disagreeable. Such were the gentle murmurs of

170
00:14:07,466 --> 00:14:11,765
Mrs. Bennet, and they gave way only to the greater distress of Mr. Bingley’s continued absence.

171
00:14:12,299 --> 00:14:17,233
Neither Jane nor Elizabeth were comfortable on this subject. Day after day passed away

172
00:14:17,233 --> 00:14:20,899
without bringing any other tidings of him than the report which shortly prevailed

173
00:14:20,899 --> 00:14:25,632
in Meryton of his coming no more to Netherfield the whole winter; a report which highly incensed

174
00:14:25,632 --> 00:14:29,966
Mrs. Bennet, and which she never failed to contradict as a most scandalous falsehood.

175
00:14:29,966 --> 00:14:34,765
Even Elizabeth began to fear—not that Bingley was indifferent—but that his

176
00:14:34,765 --> 00:14:39,299
sisters would be successful in keeping him away. Unwilling as she was to admit an idea

177
00:14:39,299 --> 00:14:43,466
so destructive to Jane’s happiness, and so dishonourable to the stability of her lover,

178
00:14:43,466 --> 00:14:48,733
she could not prevent its frequently recurring. The united efforts of his two unfeeling sisters,

179
00:14:48,733 --> 00:14:53,233
and of his overpowering friend, assisted by the attractions of Miss Darcy and the

180
00:14:53,233 --> 00:14:57,799
amusements of London, might be too much, she feared, for the strength of his attachment.

181
00:14:58,399 --> 00:15:03,733
As for Jane, her anxiety under this suspense was, of course, more painful than Elizabeth’s:

182
00:15:03,733 --> 00:15:08,066
but whatever she felt she was desirous of concealing; and between herself and Elizabeth,

183
00:15:08,066 --> 00:15:13,265
therefore, the subject was never alluded to. But as no such delicacy restrained her mother,

184
00:15:13,265 --> 00:15:18,733
an hour seldom passed in which she did not talk of Bingley, express her impatience for his arrival,

185
00:15:18,733 --> 00:15:23,399
or even require Jane to confess that if he did not come back she should think herself very

186
00:15:23,399 --> 00:15:28,933
ill-used. It needed all Jane’s steady mildness to bear these attacks with tolerable tranquillity.

187
00:15:30,132 --> 00:15:35,066
Mr. Collins returned most punctually on the Monday fortnight, but his reception at Longbourn

188
00:15:35,066 --> 00:15:40,032
was not quite so gracious as it had been on his first introduction. He was too happy, however,

189
00:15:40,032 --> 00:15:44,000
to need much attention; and, luckily for the others, the business of love-making relieved

190
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:48,566
them from a great deal of his company. The chief of every day was spent by him at Lucas Lodge,

191
00:15:48,566 --> 00:15:53,165
and he sometimes returned to Longbourn only in time to make an apology for his absence before

192
00:15:53,165 --> 00:15:54,333
the family went to bed.

193
00:15:54,333 --> 00:15:57,066
“Whenever she spoke in a low voice” Mrs. Bennet was really in a most pitiable

194
00:15:57,066 --> 00:16:02,100
state. The very mention of anything concerning the match threw her into an agony of ill-humour,

195
00:16:02,100 --> 00:16:07,632
and wherever she went she was sure of hearing it talked of. The sight of Miss Lucas was odious to

196
00:16:07,632 --> 00:16:13,032
her. As her successor in that house, she regarded her with jealous abhorrence. Whenever Charlotte

197
00:16:13,032 --> 00:16:16,799
came to see them, she concluded her to be anticipating the hour of possession;

198
00:16:16,799 --> 00:16:21,265
and whenever she spoke in a low voice to Mr. Collins, was convinced that they were talking

199
00:16:21,265 --> 00:16:24,899
of the Longbourn estate, and resolving to turn herself and her daughters out of

200
00:16:24,899 --> 00:16:29,299
the house as soon as Mr. Bennet was dead. She complained bitterly of all this to her husband.

201
00:16:30,066 --> 00:16:35,365
“Indeed, Mr. Bennet,” said she, “it is very hard to think that Charlotte Lucas should

202
00:16:35,365 --> 00:16:38,700
ever be mistress of this house, that I should be forced to make way for her,

203
00:16:38,700 --> 00:16:43,933
and live to see her take my place in it!” “My dear, do not give way to such gloomy

204
00:16:43,933 --> 00:16:49,566
thoughts. Let us hope for better things. Let us flatter ourselves that I may be the survivor.”

205
00:16:50,165 --> 00:16:53,966
This was not very consoling to Mrs. Bennet; and, therefore, instead of

206
00:16:53,966 --> 00:16:58,399
making any answer, she went on as before. “I cannot bear to think that they should

207
00:16:58,399 --> 00:17:01,799
have all this estate. If it was not for the entail, I should not mind it.”

208
00:17:02,399 --> 00:17:06,132
“What should not you mind?” “I should not mind anything at all.”

209
00:17:06,733 --> 00:17:10,399
“Let us be thankful that you are preserved from a state of such insensibility.”

210
00:17:11,100 --> 00:17:15,600
“I never can be thankful, Mr. Bennet, for anything about the entail. How anyone could

211
00:17:15,600 --> 00:17:19,399
have the conscience to entail away an estate from one’s own daughters I cannot understand;

212
00:17:19,399 --> 00:17:24,899
and all for the sake of Mr. Collins, too! Why should he have it more than anybody else?”

213
00:17:25,799 --> 00:17:29,600
“I leave it to yourself to determine,” said Mr. Bennet.

214
00:17:31,933 --> 00:17:33,066
CHAPTER XXIV.

215
00:17:40,865 --> 00:17:46,100
MISS BINGLEY’S letter arrived, and put an end to doubt. The very first sentence

216
00:17:46,100 --> 00:17:49,566
conveyed the assurance of their being all settled in London for the winter,

217
00:17:49,566 --> 00:17:54,899
and concluded with her brother’s regret at not having had time to pay his respects to his friends

218
00:17:54,899 --> 00:18:02,165
in Hertfordshire before he left the country. Hope was over, entirely over; and when Jane

219
00:18:02,165 --> 00:18:07,165
could attend to the rest of the letter, she found little, except the professed affection of

220
00:18:07,165 --> 00:18:13,799
the writer, that could give her any comfort. Miss Darcy’s praise occupied the chief of it. Her many

221
00:18:13,799 --> 00:18:19,566
attractions were again dwelt on; and Caroline boasted joyfully of their increasing intimacy,

222
00:18:19,566 --> 00:18:24,566
and ventured to predict the accomplishment of the wishes which had been unfolded in her former

223
00:18:24,566 --> 00:18:30,000
letter. She wrote also with great pleasure of her brother’s being an inmate of Mr. Darcy’s house,

224
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:34,333
and mentioned with raptures some plans of the latter with regard to new furniture.

225
00:18:35,365 --> 00:18:41,365
Elizabeth, to whom Jane very soon communicated the chief of all this, heard it in silent indignation.

226
00:18:41,365 --> 00:18:48,132
Her heart was divided between concern for her sister and resentment against all others. To

227
00:18:48,132 --> 00:18:53,600
Caroline’s assertion of her brother’s being partial to Miss Darcy, she paid no credit. That he

228
00:18:53,600 --> 00:18:59,100
was really fond of Jane, she doubted no more than she had ever done; and much as she had always been

229
00:18:59,100 --> 00:19:05,233
disposed to like him, she could not think without anger, hardly without contempt, on that easiness

230
00:19:05,233 --> 00:19:10,865
of temper, that want of proper resolution, which now made him the slave of his designing friends,

231
00:19:10,865 --> 00:19:15,299
and led him to sacrifice his own happiness to the caprice of their inclinations.

232
00:19:15,966 --> 00:19:20,100
Had his own happiness, however, been the only sacrifice, he might have been allowed

233
00:19:20,100 --> 00:19:24,865
to sport with it in whatever manner he thought best; but her sister’s was involved in it,

234
00:19:24,865 --> 00:19:30,799
as she thought he must be sensible himself. It was a subject, in short, on which reflection would

235
00:19:30,799 --> 00:19:35,466
be long indulged, and must be unavailing. She could think of nothing else; and yet,

236
00:19:35,466 --> 00:19:40,265
whether Bingley’s regard had really died away, or were suppressed by his friends’ interference;

237
00:19:40,265 --> 00:19:44,966
whether he had been aware of Jane’s attachment, or whether it had escaped his observation;

238
00:19:44,966 --> 00:19:49,865
whichever were the case, though her opinion of him must be materially affected by the difference,

239
00:19:49,865 --> 00:19:54,566
her sister’s situation remained the same, her peace equally wounded.

240
00:19:55,466 --> 00:20:00,665
A day or two passed before Jane had courage to speak of her feelings to Elizabeth; but at last,

241
00:20:00,665 --> 00:20:05,466
on Mrs. Bennet’s leaving them together, after a longer irritation than usual about Netherfield

242
00:20:05,466 --> 00:20:11,000
and its master, she could not help saying,— “O that my dear mother had more command over

243
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:17,100
herself! she can have no idea of the pain she gives me by her continual reflections on him. But

244
00:20:17,100 --> 00:20:23,100
I will not repine. It cannot last long. He will be forgot, and we shall all be as we were before.”

245
00:20:23,833 --> 00:20:28,100
Elizabeth looked at her sister with incredulous solicitude, but said nothing.

246
00:20:28,865 --> 00:20:35,532
“You doubt me,” cried Jane, slightly colouring; “indeed, you have no reason. He may live in my

247
00:20:35,532 --> 00:20:40,032
memory as the most amiable man of my acquaintance but that is all. I have

248
00:20:40,032 --> 00:20:45,600
nothing either to hope or fear, and nothing to reproach him with. Thank God I have not

249
00:20:45,600 --> 00:20:50,399
that pain. A little time, therefore—I shall certainly try to get the better——”

250
00:20:51,299 --> 00:20:57,365
With a stronger voice she soon added, “I have this comfort immediately, that it has not been

251
00:20:57,365 --> 00:21:02,833
more than an error of fancy on my side, and that it has done no harm to anyone but myself.”

252
00:21:03,799 --> 00:21:11,100
“My dear Jane,” exclaimed Elizabeth, “you are too good. Your sweetness and disinterestedness

253
00:21:11,100 --> 00:21:17,466
are really angelic; I do not know what to say to you. I feel as if I had never done you justice,

254
00:21:17,466 --> 00:21:21,733
or loved you as you deserve.” Miss Bennet eagerly disclaimed

255
00:21:21,733 --> 00:21:25,500
all extraordinary merit, and threw back the praise on her sister’s warm affection.

256
00:21:26,365 --> 00:21:32,066
“Nay,” said Elizabeth, “this is not fair. You wish to think all the world respectable,

257
00:21:32,066 --> 00:21:37,466
and are hurt if I speak ill of anybody. I only want to think you perfect, and you set yourself

258
00:21:37,466 --> 00:21:43,500
against it. Do not be afraid of my running into any excess, of my encroaching on your privilege

259
00:21:43,500 --> 00:21:49,200
of universal good-will. You need not. There are few people whom I really love, and still

260
00:21:49,200 --> 00:21:54,200
fewer of whom I think well. The more I see of the world the more am I dissatisfied with it;

261
00:21:54,200 --> 00:21:58,700
and every day confirms my belief of the inconsistency of all human characters,

262
00:21:58,700 --> 00:22:03,500
and of the little dependence that can be placed on the appearance of either merit or sense.

263
00:22:03,500 --> 00:22:09,299
I have met with two instances lately: one I will not mention, the other is Charlotte’s

264
00:22:09,299 --> 00:22:13,799
marriage. It is unaccountable! in every view it is unaccountable!”

265
00:22:13,799 --> 00:22:21,066
“My dear Lizzy, do not give way to such feelings as these. They will ruin your happiness. You do

266
00:22:21,066 --> 00:22:26,066
not make allowance enough for difference of situation and temper. Consider Mr. Collins’s

267
00:22:26,066 --> 00:22:31,165
respectability, and Charlotte’s prudent, steady character. Remember that she is one

268
00:22:31,165 --> 00:22:36,799
of a large family; that as to fortune it is a most eligible match; and be ready to believe,

269
00:22:36,799 --> 00:22:41,532
for everybody’s sake, that she may feel something like regard and esteem for our cousin.”

270
00:22:42,265 --> 00:22:46,265
“To oblige you, I would try to believe almost anything, but no one else could

271
00:22:46,265 --> 00:22:51,066
be benefited by such a belief as this; for were I persuaded that Charlotte had any

272
00:22:51,066 --> 00:22:55,466
regard for him, I should only think worse of her understanding than I now do of her heart.

273
00:22:56,066 --> 00:23:02,600
My dear Jane, Mr. Collins is a conceited, pompous, narrow-minded, silly man: you know he is,

274
00:23:02,600 --> 00:23:07,700
as well as I do; and you must feel, as well as I do, that the woman who marries him cannot have a

275
00:23:07,700 --> 00:23:13,200
proper way of thinking. You shall not defend her, though it is Charlotte Lucas. You shall not, for

276
00:23:13,200 --> 00:23:18,365
the sake of one individual, change the meaning of principle and integrity, nor endeavour to persuade

277
00:23:18,365 --> 00:23:23,700
yourself or me, that selfishness is prudence, and insensibility of danger security for happiness.”

278
00:23:24,665 --> 00:23:30,365
“I must think your language too strong in speaking of both,” replied Jane; “and I hope you will be

279
00:23:30,365 --> 00:23:35,399
convinced of it, by seeing them happy together. But enough of this. You alluded to something

280
00:23:35,399 --> 00:23:41,765
else. You mentioned two instances. I cannot misunderstand you, but I entreat you, dear Lizzy,

281
00:23:41,765 --> 00:23:47,365
not to pain me by thinking that person to blame, and saying your opinion of him is sunk. We must

282
00:23:47,365 --> 00:23:52,933
not be so ready to fancy ourselves intentionally injured. We must not expect a lively young man to

283
00:23:52,933 --> 00:23:58,865
be always so guarded and circumspect. It is very often nothing but our own vanity that deceives us.

284
00:23:58,865 --> 00:24:05,100
Women fancy admiration means more than it does.” “And men take care that they should.”

285
00:24:05,765 --> 00:24:10,566
“If it is designedly done, they cannot be justified; but I have no idea of there being so

286
00:24:10,566 --> 00:24:16,165
much design in the world as some persons imagine.” “I am far from attributing any part of Mr.

287
00:24:16,165 --> 00:24:20,700
Bingley’s conduct to design,” said Elizabeth; “but, without scheming to do wrong,

288
00:24:20,700 --> 00:24:26,100
or to make others unhappy, there may be error and there may be misery. Thoughtlessness,

289
00:24:26,100 --> 00:24:30,299
want of attention to other people’s feelings, and want of resolution, will do the business.”

290
00:24:31,466 --> 00:24:38,000
“And do you impute it to either of those?” “Yes; to the last. But if I go on I shall

291
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:42,066
displease you by saying what I think of persons you esteem. Stop me, whilst you can.”

292
00:24:42,966 --> 00:24:45,966
“You persist, then, in supposing his sisters influence him?”

293
00:24:46,566 --> 00:24:51,899
“Yes, in conjunction with his friend.” “I cannot believe it. Why should they

294
00:24:51,899 --> 00:24:55,799
try to influence him? They can only wish his happiness; and if he is

295
00:24:55,799 --> 00:25:02,165
attached to me no other woman can secure it.” “Your first position is false. They may wish many

296
00:25:02,165 --> 00:25:06,966
things besides his happiness: they may wish his increase of wealth and consequence; they may wish

297
00:25:06,966 --> 00:25:11,100
him to marry a girl who has all the importance of money, great connections, and pride.”

298
00:25:11,665 --> 00:25:17,100
“Beyond a doubt they do wish him to choose Miss Darcy,” replied Jane; “but this may be from

299
00:25:17,100 --> 00:25:21,299
better feelings than you are supposing. They have known her much longer than they have known me;

300
00:25:21,299 --> 00:25:26,399
no wonder if they love her better. But, whatever may be their own wishes, it is very unlikely

301
00:25:26,399 --> 00:25:30,665
they should have opposed their brother’s. What sister would think herself at liberty to do it,

302
00:25:30,665 --> 00:25:35,100
unless there were something very objectionable? If they believed him attached to me they would

303
00:25:35,100 --> 00:25:40,865
not try to part us; if he were so, they could not succeed. By supposing such an affection, you make

304
00:25:40,865 --> 00:25:46,799
everybody acting unnaturally and wrong, and me most unhappy. Do not distress me by the idea.

305
00:25:46,799 --> 00:25:52,500
I am not ashamed of having been mistaken—or, at least, it is slight, it is nothing in comparison

306
00:25:52,500 --> 00:25:57,132
of what I should feel in thinking ill of him or his sisters. Let me take it in the best light,

307
00:25:57,132 --> 00:26:02,466
in the light in which it may be understood.” Elizabeth could not oppose such a wish;

308
00:26:02,466 --> 00:26:06,865
and from this time Mr. Bingley’s name was scarcely ever mentioned between them.

309
00:26:07,399 --> 00:26:13,032
Mrs. Bennet still continued to wonder and repine at his returning no more; and though a day seldom

310
00:26:13,032 --> 00:26:17,399
passed in which Elizabeth did not account for it clearly, there seemed little chance of her ever

311
00:26:17,399 --> 00:26:22,165
considering it with less perplexity. Her daughter endeavoured to convince her of what she did not

312
00:26:22,165 --> 00:26:27,132
believe herself, that his attentions to Jane had been merely the effect of a common and transient

313
00:26:27,132 --> 00:26:32,233
liking, which ceased when he saw her no more; but though the probability of the statement was

314
00:26:32,233 --> 00:26:38,100
admitted at the time, she had the same story to repeat every day. Mrs. Bennet’s best comfort was,

315
00:26:38,100 --> 00:26:44,066
that Mr. Bingley must be down again in the summer. Mr. Bennet treated the matter differently. “So,

316
00:26:44,066 --> 00:26:50,299
Lizzy,” said he, one day, “your sister is crossed in love, I find. I congratulate her. Next to being

317
00:26:50,299 --> 00:26:54,799
married, a girl likes to be crossed in love a little now and then. It is something to think of,

318
00:26:54,799 --> 00:26:59,700
and gives her a sort of distinction among her companions. When is your turn to come? You will

319
00:26:59,700 --> 00:27:05,466
hardly bear to be long outdone by Jane. Now is your time. Here are officers enough at Meryton

320
00:27:05,466 --> 00:27:10,700
to disappoint all the young ladies in the country. Let Wickham be your man. He is a pleasant fellow,

321
00:27:10,700 --> 00:27:14,966
and would jilt you creditably.” “Thank you, sir, but a less agreeable

322
00:27:14,966 --> 00:27:19,399
man would satisfy me. We must not all expect Jane’s good fortune.”

323
00:27:19,399 --> 00:27:24,966
“True,” said Mr. Bennet; “but it is a comfort to think that, whatever of that kind may befall you,

324
00:27:24,966 --> 00:27:27,865
you have an affectionate mother who will always make the most of it.”

325
00:27:28,933 --> 00:27:33,500
Mr. Wickham’s society was of material service in dispelling the gloom which the late perverse

326
00:27:33,500 --> 00:27:37,566
occurrences had thrown on many of the Longbourn family. They saw him often,

327
00:27:37,566 --> 00:27:43,100
and to his other recommendations was now added that of general unreserve. The whole of what

328
00:27:43,100 --> 00:27:47,899
Elizabeth had already heard, his claims on Mr. Darcy, and all that he had suffered from him,

329
00:27:47,899 --> 00:27:52,566
was now openly acknowledged and publicly canvassed; and everybody was pleased to think

330
00:27:52,566 --> 00:27:56,765
how much they had always disliked Mr. Darcy before they had known anything of the matter.

331
00:27:57,733 --> 00:28:00,665
Miss Bennet was the only creature who could suppose there might be

332
00:28:00,665 --> 00:28:04,933
any extenuating circumstances in the case unknown to the society of Hertfordshire:

333
00:28:04,933 --> 00:28:10,799
her mild and steady candour always pleaded for allowances, and urged the possibility of mistakes;

334
00:28:10,799 --> 00:28:15,600
but by everybody else Mr. Darcy was condemned as the worst of men.

335
00:28:17,966 --> 00:28:19,032
CHAPTER XXV.
